---
source: crates/hibi-cli/tests/snapshots.rs
expression: "help(&repo, &[command, \"--help\"])"
---
$ hibi claim --help
exit status: 0
---- standard output ----
Record a claim: a statement, the unit it is about (the subject) and the units that support it (the evidence). hibi stores the claim and a first mark with the fingerprint of every side as it is now. Run this after you checked that the statement is true.

Every anchor must match exactly one unit. Running the same claim again adds a new mark and no second claim.

With --stdin, standard input is a JSON array of claims, written all or not at all:
  [{"statement": "...", "subject": "file:docs/a.md::quoted text",
    "evidence": ["file:src/a.rs#name"], "recheck": "P90D",
    "note": "...", "by": "..."}]

An anchor is a compact string or an object {"loc": ..., "sel": {...}}. Only "statement" and "subject" are required. `hibi schema claim-input` prints the schema.

Usage: hibi claim [OPTIONS] [statement]

Arguments:
  [statement]
          The statement that was checked

Options:
      --subject <anchor>
          What the statement is about: an anchor, usually the sentence in a document that makes the statement

      --evidence <anchor>
          A unit that supports the statement: an anchor, usually a function or another declaration. Repeat for several

      --recheck <duration>
          Check again after this long even if nothing changed: an ISO 8601 duration with whole numbers, for example P90D or P6M

      --note <text>
          A note stored with the claim: how to check it again, or where the truth lives

      --by <text>
          Who checked, stored with the mark for the record [default: the value of HIBI_BY]

      --stdin
          Read a JSON array of claims from standard input. No other claim argument is allowed with it

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --root <dir>
          Use this directory as the root (the directory that contains .hibi/) instead of searching upward from the working directory

      --format <format>
          Output format. Without this option the output is JSON when standard output is not a terminal, and text when it is

          Possible values:
          - human: Text for a person
          - json:  One JSON object

      --now <timestamp>
          Use this instant as the current time, for reproducible runs. RFC 3339 with any offset, for example 2026-11-20T18:04:11Z. A fraction of a second is dropped

      --no-cache
          Do not read or write the cache database. Results are the same with and without the cache
